A1. Give this a twist and you'll get clear around the world. ~ GLOBE
A6. Millions and millions and millions. ~ SCADS
A11. Just vacillating around. ~ SHILLYSHALLYING
A16. A wild party. ~ HORDE
A17. This moves smartly along Park Avenue. ~ SET
A18. The code that's liable to keep a young man from forging right ahead. ~ PENAL
A19. Fly-by-nights. ~ OWLS
A20. Back in Rome she controlled the corn market. ~ CERES
A22. The guy who holds the bag. ~ DUPE
A23. To move like a ferryboat. ~ PLY
A24. This goes round in motor cars. ~ FANBELT
A26. Shore kisser. ~ SEA
A27. Exactly two-fifths of seven. ~ SE
A28. This turns piccaninnies pale. ~ TALC
A29. This means food and drink for the working classes. ~ NOON
A31. A room without those things you can look thru. ~ RM
A32. To draw the line again. ~ RERULE
A34. The kind of girl who always gets her man. ~ SPEEDY
A36. A little change for a Roman. ~ AES
A37. An unsocial lion. ~ LEO
A38. Wagged the chin incessantly. ~ PRATED
A41. Match makers. ~ SWEDES
A44. Ritzy for "pappy" (abbr.) ~ SR
A45. This is the air. ~ TUNE
A46. You'll have to do this to seal a girl's lips. ~ KISS
A47. An 'arf and 'arf (abbr.) ~ PT
A49. A throat gurgle. ~ COO
A51. Old hens. ~ SITTERS
A53. To apply heat to fish by means of iron. ~ FRY
A54. The kind of seaman that gets around the girls. ~ ABLE
A56. What Boston censors do to piano legs. ~ DRAPE
A57. This often goes over a girl's head. ~ SLIP
A58. This is a sticker. ~ LEECH
A60. We sometimes wonder why this is hung in place of the artist. ~ ART
A61. The way Chicago gangsters prefer going for a ride. ~ ALONE
A62. These people are all wet and don't know it. ~ PROHIBITIONISTS
A65. Big shot. ~ SOLON
A66. Some reasons why young men go to Cornell. ~ COEDS

D1. Little Womanish. ~ GIRLY
D2. Bartenders (abbr.) ~ LLDS
D3. Old Bill's private den. ~ OLE
D4. If you say this you're bound to say "Golly !" ~ BY
D5. This is the gist of It. ~ ESSENCE
D6. The goods you get on a woman. ~ SATEENS
D7. 150. ~ CL
D8. Carnera-sized mountain. ~ ALP
D9. When he did this he changed color several shades. ~ DYED
D10. Pleasant trouble for the doctors. ~ SINUS
D11. Woman traps. ~ SHOPS
D12. What Pres. Hoover was when a baby. ~ HOWLER
D13. The fat Cal. ~ HERB
D14. This comes clean to the table. ~ NAPERY
D15. What does Broadway do at night? ~ GLEAM
D20. This is just an old plug to a telephone girl. ~ CALL
D21. It's bad to kick this kind of bucket. ~ SLOP
D24. He went to the devil years ago but wound up an operatic favorite. ~ FAUSTUS
D25. You'll never get a good kick out of this sort of man. ~ TOELESS
D28. Verb. (rare, Scottish). ~ TREAT
D30. The poor are known for these. ~ NEEDS
D33. Anyway, when a man asks for a loan, you can always lend him this. ~ EAR
D35. This lady is always welcome at stag parties. ~ DOE
D38. This gets to the inside of things. ~ PROBER
D39. One of Tennyson's women. (No scandal attached.) ~ ENID
D40. What you do when you're thru using the railroad. ~ DETRAIN
D41. This man would make an ideal defense man on any football team—he never lets anything get past him. ~ SKEPTIC
D42. Electrified messenger. ~ WIRE
D43. A short run for your money. ~ SPRINT
D44. Hair raiser. ~ SCALP
D48. Just a lot of set-ups for the printer. ~ TYPES
D50. Greasers. ~ OLEOS
D52. What the Queen of Hearts made. ~ TART
D53. A toothsome line. ~ FLOSS
D55. A noisy comeback. ~ ECHO
D57. How Kelly worked his way into fame. ~ SLID
D59. Two-thirds the way up a high place. ~ HIL
D61. A Scotch tip often runs into this many figures. ~ ANE
